===Noun===
{{en-noun|~}}

# {{lb|en|finance}} A [[security]] or [[guarantee]] (usually an [[asset]]) pledged for the repayment of a [[loan]] if one cannot procure enough [[fund]]s to repay.
#: {{syn|en|collateral security|pledge}}
#* {{quote-web|en|year=2016|author=Ambrose Evans-Pritchard|work=The Telegraph|url=http://www.telegraph.co.uk/business/2016/10/16/euro-house-of-cards-to-collapse-warns-ecb-prophet/|title=Euro 'house of cards' to collapse, warns ECB prophet|passage="The decline in the quality of eligible '''collateral''' is a grave problem. The ECB is now buying corporate bonds that are close to junk, and the haircuts can barely deal with a one-notch credit downgrade. The reputational risk of such actions by a central bank would have been unthinkable in the past."}}
#* {{quote-web|en|url=https://www.nytimes.com/interactive/2019/08/14/magazine/slavery-capitalism.html|title=In order to understand the brutality of American capitalism, you have to start on the plantation|author=Matthew Desmond|work=New York Times|date=2019-08-14|passage= In colonial times, when land was not worth much and banks didn’t exist, most lending was based on human property. In the early 1700s, slaves were the dominant '''collateral''' in South Carolina.}}
# {{lb|en|now|_|rare|genealogy}} A collateral (not [[linear]]) family member.
# {{lb|en|anatomy}} A [[branch]] of a bodily part or system of organs.
#: {{ux|en|Besides the arteries blood streams through numerous veins we call '''collaterals'''.}}
# {{lb|en|marketing}} Printed materials or content of electronic media used to enhance sales of products (short form of [[collateral material]]).
# {{lb|en|anatomy}} A thinner [[blood vessel]] providing an alternate route to blood flow in case the main vessel becomes occluded.
# {{lb|en|archaic}} A [[contemporary]] or [[rival]].

